Every file after the SVG is a derivative work of it, generated by
tools/gen_icons.py, and carries exactly the same restriction. The
generator script itself is code and is under the Artistic License. So
are the two small XML files beside the Android rasters: an adaptive-icon
layer list and a single hex colour lifted from the drawing, neither of
which is artwork.

Each of these is declared in machine-readable form, because the first
thing a packager or a compliance tool does is scan the tree, and an
unlabelled file in a repository with one LICENSE at its root reads as
being under it. The desktop files each have an SPDX sidecar
(`<file>.license`). **The Android rasters cannot**: everything under
`res/` is fed to the Android resource merger, which fails the build on
any file not named `*.xml` or `*.png`. They are covered instead by
native/android-app/REUSE.toml, which is REUSE's mechanism for exactly
that case and is generated by the same script.

What this means if you redistribute
-----------------------------------

**Replace the branding.** A fork, a distribution package, or any build
you publish must substitute its own icon. This is the same arrangement
Firefox, Chrome and Visual Studio Code have with their open-source
builds: the code is free, the brand is not.

A `SSTVAE_BRANDING` build switch to make that a one-line change, with a
freely-licensed placeholder icon, is planned but **not implemented yet**
— see docs/todo.md. Until it exists, replacing the files listed above in
place is the way to do it; nothing reads them by any other path.

Binaries published from this repository by the project itself do include
the licensed artwork, which the license permits.

